[Music]  days after causing devastation and  killing at least 67  people in the philippines typhoon  vancouver has made landfall in vietnam  ramco had already weakened when it hit  but still caused serious damage and  injured several people  picking up the pieces quite literally in  the northern vietnamese city of huey  the storm destroyed many buildings there  blowing off roofs  and uprooting trees it lost strength  since moving on from the philippines but  still  caused significant damage  the authorities prepared people for the  onslaught as the storm approached  evacuating nearly 650 000 people in  coastal areas to higher ground  famco is the latest in a series of  storms to cause disruption  and misery in vietnam at least 159  people have been killed in floods and  landslides in the last six weeks  in the philippines storm vamco has  exacted a heavy toll  with a high number of casualties  especially in the north of the country  authorities are now assessing the scale  of the damage  some communities are describing it as  the worst flooding in four decades  many villages were swamped crops  destroyed  and hundreds of thousands left without  power  as in vietnam the philippines has also  been battered relentlessly by storms  over the last two months  bamko is the eighth to hit the country  in the last eight weeks  and the 21st this year  but for all the drama of the last week  the hope in the philippines is that the  worst is over  at least for now until the next storm  hits  and joining me now is dr omero paltan  from the university of  oxford a part of dr paltan's research  includes climate change  and its impact on communities dr palton  welcome  what is causing these increasingly  devastating storms  in the region  yeah thank you very much for having me  here today so  yeah i think there's a connection with  climate change while the  direct again link has not been yet uh  quantified i think that we have to have  it cleared so  that as global temperature increase so  the ocean temperatures  so yeah and then warmer oceans are  expected to aggravate the intensity and  impacts of  tropical storms as we are seeing now uh  well i would also like to  make it clear that unfortunately the  connection between climate change and  frequency of storms is not very strong  yet  so when you say that the ocean waters  are warming  what you're essentially saying is that  storms in the future  are going to get more powerful  yeah indeed that uh that's a and that's  what climate science is telling us at  present  so we have uh yeah we were expected to  see that in the coming years  storms uh more intense but it's also  important to  to see that we would also expect to see  changes in the global water cycle  due to climate change or basically the  way that it rains  so climate models and climate science  for the region in generally agree  that the region will be expected to see  more floods  for more intense precipitation events  not just from from  from tropical storms so what i think  this has been very evident during these  last  months where we have seen where we have  seen a very intense uh  or unusually strong monsoon season  and so yeah i would also like to  express that uh climb  even though we're expected to see more  flooding people should not be surprised  if in the next years or even the next  year  we just see a monsoon season that is  relatively weak or rainfall  comes late in general i would say that  climate change  means a more variable and a more or more  unexpected  climate patterns right  in terms of these unexpected climate  patterns and  storms getting more powerful what does  this mean for people  is there any preparation that  governments and communities  can do to avoid the devastation from  these storms  yeah definitely so in general a there  should be  overall integrative strategies that look  that should look at enhancing the  resilience of not just infrastructure  infrastructure systems as traditional  dams dikes green infrastructure as in  mangroves forests etc but also  strategies to look the uh the resilience  of communities of people  so that means that people should get  used or adapted to  more of these unexpected again  water climate patterns and then at the  at a greater scale i would say that  institutions a at the government level  local or national levels  should be more prepared to face this  type of of  challenges we'll leave it there for the  time being dr  omero paltan from the university of  oxford thank you

